We are going to expore the builtin functions of SQL for String and other numerical data types. 
The first type is String data type. 
We are learning Padding for strings. 
there will be scenarios while working with clients that they expect 20 characters in teh UI so we need to pull the data in a way that returns 20 character string so that is where we use Padding. 
Left padding and right padding. 

SELECT title, RPAD(title, 20, '*') AS left_padded
FROM sakila.film
LIMIT 5;

SELECT title, LPAD(RPAD(title, 20, '*'),20,'*') AS left_padded
FROM sakila.film
LIMIT 5;

there is also somehting called SUBSTRING function

SELECT title, SUBSTRING(title, 1, 9) AS short_title
FROM sakila.film;

CONCAT is another builtin function for concatenation 

SELECT CONCAT(first_name, '@', last_name) AS full_name
FROM sakila.customer;

Then we have the REVERSE builtin function 

SELECT title, REVERSE(title) AS reversed_title
FROM sakila.film
LIMIT 5;

Then we have the length builtin function

SELECT title, LENGTH(title) AS title_length
FROM sakila.film
WHERE LENGTH(title)=8;

One more builtin function which is LOCATE

SELECT email, 
        SUBSTRING(email, LOCATE('@', email)+1) AS domain
FROM sakila.customer;

we also have builtin functions like string_index and also UPPER and LOWER for upper case and lower case.

Two more built in functions like LEFT and RIGHT to get the leftmost or rightmost characters of the string.

SELECT LEFT(TITLE, 2) AS FIRST_LETTER, RIGHT(TITLE, 3) AS LAST_LETTER, COUNT(*) AS FILM_COUNT
FROM SAKIA.FILM
GROUP BY LEFT(TITLE, 2), RIGHT(TITLE,3)
ORDER BY FILM_COUNT DESC;


the next concept of CASE condition where the condition is satisfied. 

![alt text](image.png)


SELECT LAST_NAME,
    CASE
        WHEN LEFT(LAST_NAME, 1) BETWEEN 'A' AND 'M' THEN 'GROUP A-M'
        WHEN LEFT(LAST_NAME, 1) BETWEEN 'N' AND 'Z' THEN 'GROUP N-Z'
        ESLE 'OTHER'
    END AS GROUP_LABEL
FROM SAKILA.CUSTOMER;

